13:06:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: # GPS Is a One-Way Broadcast System GPS handles unlimited users because **satellites only transmit—they never receive** signals from your device. ## How It Actually Works 1. **Satellites broadcast continuously**: Each GPS satellite constantly sends out radio signals containing its precise location and the exact time. 2. **Your device only listens**: Your phone, car, or GPS unit passively receives these signals. It doesn't send anything back to the satellites. 3. **Your device does the math**: By comparing the time it took signals from multiple satellites (usually 4+) to reach you, your device calculates its own position using trilateration—essentially figuring out "if I'm this many milliseconds from satellite A, this many from satellite B..." and so on. ## Why This Scales Infinitely Think of it like a radio station broadcasting music: - One radio tower can serve unlimited listeners - The tower doesn't know or care how many radios are tuned in - Each radio just picks up the signal independently GPS works the same way. Whether 10 people or 10 billion people are using GPS simultaneously, it makes zero difference to the satellites—they're just broadcasting the same signal regardless. ## The Real Constraint The limiting factor isn't the satellites' capacity, but rather: - **Signal strength** (need clear sky view) - **Number of satellites visible** from your location (need at least 4 for a 3D fix) - **Your device's processing power** to calculate position from the signals This is fundamentally different from something like cell phone networks, where each connection requires two-way communication and the tower does have a practical limit on simultaneous connections.
