tric coverage (especially at high latitudes or in valleys), improving accuracy and reducing single-point-of-failure risk—again, a quality/reliability upgrade, not a capacity one. 4. **Military modernization schedule**: GPS is fundamentally a US military system (civilian access is a byproduct). Satellite upgrades follow defense modernization budgets and geopolitical timelines—not FCC-style "we need more towers because of subscriber growth" logic. ## The Real Coincidence The timing similarity you noticed is real, but it's because **both** things (satellite modernization and civilian GPS explosion) trace back to the same root cause: **the 1990s-2000s were when GPS matured from a military-only system into a dual-use civilian technology**. That maturation naturally involved *both* more satellites (better signals, security, coverage) *and* more users (once accuracy improved and devices got cheap)—but neither one caused the other. They're sibling effects of the same underlying push, not cause-and-effect.
