13:10:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right that this was taught, and I don't think that lesson was wrong—it's just describing a **different category of satellite** than GPS. ## The Distinction: Communication Satellites vs. Navigation Satellites **What you learned in school almost certainly applies to communication satellites** (like TV/radio relay satellites, satellite internet, or satellite phones)—and for those, "user capacity" is a completely real and correctly-taught concept. Here's why: **communication satellites are transponders**. They: - Receive a signal from the ground - Amplify it - Retransmit it to another location This is fundamentally a **two-way, relay-based system**. Each satellite has a fixed number of transponders/channels, and each active user/call/stream occupies one of those channels. This is *exactly* like a cell tower—there's a hard capacity limit, and satellite internet services (like Starlink, HughesNet, satellite phones) genuinely do get congested when too many users pile onto the same satellite's bandwidth. **GPS satellites are fundamentally different**: they don't relay anything, they don't have transponders picking up ground signals, and they don't allocate channels to users. They just continuously broadcast a signal (like a lighthouse), and your receiver passively listens. ## Why This Distinction Matters | | Communication Satellites | GPS Satellites | |---|---|---| | Direction | Two-way (uplink + downlink) | One-way (broadcast only) | | Mechanism | Receives, amplifies, relays signals | Just transmits pre-generated signal | | Capacity concept | Real—limited channels/transponders | Doesn't apply—no channels to fill | | User impact | More users = more strain | More users = zero difference | ## Why the Conflation Happens "Satellite" is a broad category, and most people's mental model of satellites (rightly) comes from things like satellite TV, satellite phones, or Starlink—**all of which are communication satellites with real capacity limits**. GPS gets grouped into "satellites" colloquially, but architecturally it belongs in the same category as **broadcast radio/TV towers**, not cell towers or satellite modems. So you're not misremembering—you're just applying a true lesson about one category of satellite technology to a different category where it doesn't hold, because both get called "satellites" in casual language despite working on completely different principles.
